#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/id #uuid "4b092f86-cf53-51b4-9f79-b43d58c8b2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5563" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n![](images/7e/7e263f3e5b4f2491f2f5c469a3a0f861bf2985e67fec67d3016f4bb7b53f4493.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n![](images/1a/1a2b4f5d6fb8842115feb6e6f12ac70399768c94006966838c3817eabd85e7fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=16+16=32\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "af1ca39e-5cb7-5a93-b88f-0ff86a563bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5564" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\n![](images/b6/b62da31df7bea27c23f8dfcf1615ae9514ed48727e8503a389abc9a55ce1d108.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector ![](images/95/95c6967179c825684229dcdd991bc0ba13d66d2f7fa44d3a4f62b9c54092e229.png) such that\n\n![](images/41/410f07f748fa4e40decedadacd97ad7df0b5f9201c53db8f4934fd3096aea470.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](images/04/0477864853171cf0afdcc87e2debb27c24efa8b6166cc396d8f7cd0a126f7ace.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n![](images/a6/a6e3e898672afd315ae56af8a6e098565947b3cf8c7c2e86792e27e8e8775cdb.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](images/87/87b1cb524aee7db1f7e6f2a34c81874d2f9dae66d94a4e7b8b5c63b3713d9da7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133652 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "7ef88417-f089-51ea-9fac-dccef5825016" :question/math-academy-id "e-7114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n![](images/96/969339570bc776ebf69a7f6e9cbff0377fa35842a913e68680d8bd2ed0a1decb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$ and\n\n![](images/e7/e7034bdd15c1adc9aca80284bb2ab9b5a99ce5f33fa65f7b867c542158727b9d.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e6/e690911497bbee717323b680e92c57a7ab9d6d9d78d6ed3440d2657cd5883c3d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we obtain the vector \n![](images/53/53cd895629e3c850067d8e443c5f7ce411d1a9358a6123bc72465fdce673f14c.png) \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n![](images/86/86d8735a9101ad90efe6c83e21ba6849f488baf3202ff549e9aea92764019c0e.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](images/24/24cd2c6c9a445179df93ecf534fbab1db3906a6db78473505ea0fd6c7de2e466.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133658 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/id #uuid "8a41b0e9-c029-5175-80c9-5d9c6c1dd5f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7257" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](images/8f/8f316d4e8c33c1d8d087f2fb42e283169919a4cbc9c14a67792df70a447a41b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71774489-cfbf-54bd-8f90-2a0bee4d4a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370896} {:answer/id #uuid "424428ba-e984-56dd-a585-b888435dd669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370897} {:answer/id #uuid "afab60d1-07e3-5634-8d3e-3874475f81cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370898} {:answer/id #uuid "843220f0-6e4a-5d90-a3c6-7c18b6a664c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370899} {:answer/id #uuid "82727867-c8e6-5d02-8aeb-92e6b06797e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "843220f0-6e4a-5d90-a3c6-7c18b6a664c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370899} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e65092d-d4cf-5a8c-aa82-624d90e5b582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75f96dd3-b802-5102-880f-b4c303f55070" :question/math-academy-id "q-39477" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{26}{5} & \\frac{38}{5} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} & ={x}_{3}-2(\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}) \\\\ 5{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{25}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=13$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=-\\frac{5}{19}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29720cd0-05b4-5b72-aa7f-f9e9adfe346c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348808} {:answer/id #uuid "572eb01c-3fd8-52d3-92f3-8f8417886a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349247} {:answer/id #uuid "aef735ba-fa87-5c65-bc31-399cb2154f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349993} {:answer/id #uuid "bf771832-5ba4-5289-94b3-720cfc8a578c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350148} {:answer/id #uuid "f0abb535-abdc-5adc-b02e-f70943d024cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29720cd0-05b4-5b72-aa7f-f9e9adfe346c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348808} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d977f1-8559-526b-8a29-72ca4192caf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25a9c5a2-c2b4-580a-a454-817fa98a8c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81109" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f37115-979c-5246-9d94-c67fef08cc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348529} {:answer/id #uuid "0be4924f-2bcb-5e68-bb91-0b202ac2e3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348547} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2caaa8-3b3b-5917-9117-2c00ca744780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348569} {:answer/id #uuid "36e30243-1bcb-54b4-a974-3246d1b94ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348940} {:answer/id #uuid "93af1e85-3ecc-55b6-b207-b559abeda572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e30243-1bcb-54b4-a974-3246d1b94ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348940} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb859c54-ca8c-559d-b875-da4c3b754acf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69162096-003d-5b5d-8f8d-d7d2695037b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
